<header><p class="st-kicker">other · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Frisia</h1><p class="st-meta">1498 CE – 1581 CE · capital Leeuwarden</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Friesland west of the Ems under foreign lords after the end of the Frisian Freedom, passing from Saxony to Charles V and then into the Dutch Revolt.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1498 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Maximilian</a> named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-iii-duke-of-saxony">Albert of Saxony</a> hereditary governor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/frisian-freedom">Friesland</a>, ending the Frisian Freedom west of the Ems. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> troops imposed their rule with difficulty against Frisian resistance and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> government brought new courts, including the Hof van <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/frisian-freedom">Friesland</a> founded in 1499, and taxes, resented by many <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/frisii">Frisians</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-duke-of-guelders">Charles of Egmond</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-guelders">Guelders</a> encouraged revolt, and the rebel leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grutte-pier">Grutte Pier</a> raided the coasts in the 1510s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-of-saxony">George of Saxony</a> sold his rights to the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1515, and from 1524 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> stadtholders ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeuwarden">Leeuwarden</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/frisian-freedom">Friesland</a> joined the Revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II</a>, signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-utrecht">Union of Utrecht</a> in 1580, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-abjuration">Act of Abjuration</a> of 1581 became a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, with its own stadtholders from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau">Nassau</a> family.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-frisia.jpg" alt="Lordship of Frisia" width="267" height="303"><figcaption>Image: Johann Gustav Droysen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herrschaft_Friesland_1524.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1498 CE – 1581 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Leeuwarden</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Albert of Saxony, George of Saxony, Charles V</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>West Frisian and Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Heerlijkheid Friesland, Friesland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1540&amp;focus=lordship-of-frisia" aria-label="Lordship of Frisia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4809 347 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Frisia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5129 603L5102 664M5129 603L5099 670M5129 603L5097 634"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5102 664h0M5099 670h0M5097 634h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5129 603h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5129 603h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Leeuwarden. <a href="/atlas?year=1540&amp;focus=lordship-of-frisia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
